<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Lusatia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> is a historical region on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>, today divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, it was long held by the Bohemian crown, while its six leading towns formed the Lusatian League in 1346. It passed to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in 1635 and remains home to a Sorbian minority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia.jpg" alt="Upper Lusatia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J.-H. Janßen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bautzen_Hammermuehle_44.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
